 $\ell_1$-norm principal component analysis (L1-PCA) is frequently employed due to its robustness against anomalous data points. However, conventional L1-PCA formulations often yield sparse projections of individual data points rather than a sparse global coefficient matrix used to transform the entire dataset. This study introduces a novel extension to standard L1-PCA by defining the objective as the minimization of the $\ell_1$ norm of this principal component. To address the resulting optimization challenge, we develop a novel alternating direction method of multipliers (ADM2) algorithm. We demonstrate that the proposed method converges to a unique global solution. Furthermore, in three-dimensional space, the algorithm geometrically characterizes the solution by identifying an inscribed regular simplex within a polyhedron. Finally, we confirm through numerical experiments that the proposed approach effectively reduces dataset dimensions while remaining robust against outliers.

Principal component analysis (PCA) remains a widely used technique for reducing the dimensionality of high-dimensional data. Despite its popularity, standard PCA lacks sparsity and is highly sensitive to outliers because it relies on the $\ell_2$-norm, which is non-separable and difficult to optimize. To overcome these limitations, $\ell_1$-norm PCA was introduced, offering the advantage of sparse loading vectors that facilitate easy variable selection and interpretation. Nevertheless, traditional $\ell_1$-norm PCA still fails to produce sparse solutions for the coefficient matrix. To further enhance the sparsity of this matrix and increase robustness against outliers, we propose a new optimization model termed L1-PCA2. We also present an efficient algorithm based on the ADM2 method to solve the equivalent formulation of L1-PCA2. Numerical results indicate that the proposed approach outperforms existing methods in sparse dimensionality reduction. Keywords: $\ell_1$-norm principal component analysis; sparsity; dimensionality reduction; ADM2; geometric characterization. Mathematics subject classifications: 62H05, 62F10, 90C22, 90C31, 90C56.

